                    <section id="choose-one-habit-for-this-week" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">Choose One Habit For This Week</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>If your day starts scattered, try this: after opening your work calendar, spend five minutes choosing the next task and when to do it. Record <strong>Daily Planning</strong> as Yes / No in HabitKit. Use a weekly streak goal of five if it fits your working week; it counts any five successful days, not named weekdays. Review the record on Friday and adjust the cue if you kept forgetting.</p>
<p>The five ideas below are alternatives you can choose from. You do not need to adopt all of them. The free app supports four active habits; the tracking types used here are free.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="1-create-a-phone-free-work-session" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">1. Create A Phone-Free Work Session</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>Choose a task and a short period when you can put your phone out of reach. Keep it in another room if your work allows that, then bring it back when the session ends.</p>
<p><strong>HabitKit tip:</strong> Create <strong>Phone-Free Work Sessions</strong> as a <strong>Build</strong> habit. Under <strong>Tracking</strong>, choose <strong>Count</strong>, then set a manageable <strong>Daily Goal</strong>. Log each session after it ends. <a href="/help/habits-and-streaks/set-daily-goals-and-multiple-completions">Daily goals and multiple completions</a> explains the controls.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="2-plan-the-days-work" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">2. Plan The Day&#039;s Work</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>Before starting work, decide which tasks deserve time and put them in your calendar. Leave room for interruptions and adjust the plan when the day changes.</p>
<p><strong>HabitKit tip:</strong> Create <strong>Daily Planning</strong> with <strong>Tracking</strong> set to <strong>Yes / No</strong>. Check it off after planning. HabitKit records whether you planned; keep the schedule itself in your calendar.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="3-make-time-for-focused-work" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">3. Make Time For Focused Work</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>Give one demanding task your attention for a period that fits your day. Start with a duration you can repeat, and review whether longer sessions would actually help.</p>
<p><strong>HabitKit tip:</strong> Use <strong>Count</strong> for the number of <strong>Focused Work Sessions</strong>. If minutes are more useful, choose <strong>Measure</strong>, enter <strong>minutes</strong> as the <strong>Unit</strong>, and set a <strong>Daily Goal</strong>. Enter the amount you completed afterwards; the habit records the amount you enter rather than timing the session.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="4-set-aside-some-time-offline" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">4. Set Aside Some Time Offline</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>Choose a period each week for something you enjoy away from non-essential screens. It could be an afternoon, a walk or a meal. Make the boundary specific enough that you know when you have kept it.</p>
<p><strong>HabitKit tip:</strong> Add <strong>Time Offline</strong> with <strong>Tracking</strong> set to <strong>Yes / No</strong>. Set <strong>Streak Goal</strong> to <strong>Week</strong> and <strong>Completions Per Interval</strong> to <strong>1</strong>, then check off the day you do it. The weekly target needs one successful day, so the other days do not count as missed daily goals.</p>
</div>
</section>
                    <section id="5-review-the-week" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">5. Review The Week</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>Set aside a few minutes to look at what worked, what got in the way and one thing to change next week. Use the record to adjust your plan rather than judge the week by its longest streak.</p>
<p><strong>HabitKit tip:</strong> Create <strong>Weekly Review</strong> with <strong>Tracking</strong> set to <strong>Yes / No</strong> and a <strong>Week</strong> streak goal of <strong>1</strong>. Check off the review day and save your observations in its day editor. If the note field is hidden, enable <strong>Notes</strong> in <strong>Settings › General</strong>. <a href="/help/getting-started/log-a-completion-and-fix-a-past-day">Logging a completion and editing past days</a> covers recording and editing entries.</p>
<p><img src="https://habitkit.app/blog-assets/charcoal-day-note.webp" alt="HabitKit day editor with a completed Sport entry and the note prompt What went well? What got in the way?" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></p>
<p><em>The day editor has a place to record what helped and what got in the way. This demo uses Sport; the same note field works for a review habit.</em></p>
<p>Try one of these for a week, then decide whether it earned a place in your routine. <a href="/help/guides/start-smaller-than-feels-worthwhile">Start smaller than feels worthwhile</a> has more on choosing a target you can repeat.</p>
</div>
</section>
        
        
        
        
    </article>

